<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a memory leak in the `http_get_file_shared` function within the `clib` project. Specifically, the function allocates a `CURL` handle using `curl_easy_init()` but fails to release it with `curl_easy_cleanup()` before returning. This results in a memory leak on every invocation of the function.

To fix this, `curl_easy_cleanup(req)` should be called before the function returns. It should be called after `curl_easy_perform` and before returning from the function, ensuring that the `CURL` handle is always cleaned up, regardless of the success or failure of the HTTP request.

```c
// in /src/clib/deps/http-get/http-get.c

int http_get_file_shared(const char *url, const char *file, CURLSH *share) {
CURL *req = curl_easy_init();
if (!req) return -1;

FILE *fp = fopen(file, "wb");
if (!fp) {
curl_easy_cleanup(req); // Add cleanup
return -1;
}

if (share) {
curl_easy_setopt(req, CURLOPT_SHARE, share);
}

curl_easy_setopt(req, CURLOPT_URL, url);
curl_easy_setopt(req, CURLOPT_HTTPGET, 1);
curl_easy_setopt(req, CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION, 1);
curl_easy_setopt(req, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, http_get_file_cb);
curl_easy_setopt(req, CURLOPT_WRITEDATA, fp);
int res = curl_easy_perform(req);

long status;
curl_easy_getinfo(req, CURLINFO_RESPONSE_CODE, &status);

fclose(fp); // Close the file pointer
curl_easy_cleanup(req); // Add cleanup

if (200 != status) {
// maybe remove the file
return -1;
}

return 0 == res ? 0 : -1;
}
```
</crash-analysis>

